[{:db/id 17592186192807 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b46ac655-a97e-50cb-a022-2af841ed15e8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192535 :question/problem "![](images/15/1562da3da10e26ab9b4aff6adaa71fcbb1c2de963b4bfc682663f7fcfa62f8d6.svg)\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\geq 3$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{\\infty} f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192761 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-53d894bf-6308-5f50-b4d9-b3439fc6a9bc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192480 :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n![](images/48/48acd7d6ad8f581ba6033e19282fca0e43019c06ec2812c0698e50edd81075d6.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192479 :question/problem "Compute $P\\left( X > 8 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n![](images/85/8507c337ea73575abbc2127507f2b0ae8440b3587bf0191fe7344b8be6dfb1b3.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b46ac655-a97e-50cb-a022-2af841ed15e8" :question/id #uuid "b46ac655-a97e-50cb-a022-2af841ed15e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160451" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d77bfd50-03ce-5cb3-a091-5ba8b32f073a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d77bfd50-03ce-5cb3-a091-5ba8b32f073a" :answer-field/id #uuid "d77bfd50-03ce-5cb3-a091-5ba8b32f073a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6249b9e8-0127-5eee-876d-bd9fe3be9480" :answer-field/choices ["answer-25f82104-dc7d-57c3-98d6-01a9c65196e7" "answer-322e2b30-d710-51d2-8606-8bee18140367" "answer-59ad5255-5a70-5c33-9335-d0a5e5d59557" "answer-6249b9e8-0127-5eee-876d-bd9fe3be9480" "answer-a1d72fdb-d63f-5f10-a2b0-b041b91327b2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25f82104-dc7d-57c3-98d6-01a9c65196e7" :answer/id #uuid "25f82104-dc7d-57c3-98d6-01a9c65196e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-322e2b30-d710-51d2-8606-8bee18140367" :answer/id #uuid "322e2b30-d710-51d2-8606-8bee18140367" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59ad5255-5a70-5c33-9335-d0a5e5d59557" :answer/id #uuid "59ad5255-5a70-5c33-9335-d0a5e5d59557" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6249b9e8-0127-5eee-876d-bd9fe3be9480" :answer/id #uuid "6249b9e8-0127-5eee-876d-bd9fe3be9480" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1d72fdb-d63f-5f10-a2b0-b041b91327b2" :answer/id #uuid "a1d72fdb-d63f-5f10-a2b0-b041b91327b2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-53d894bf-6308-5f50-b4d9-b3439fc6a9bc" :question/id #uuid "53d894bf-6308-5f50-b4d9-b3439fc6a9bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-87239" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{-8x},\\, & x>0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-8x}$ on the interval $0<x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x={[-\\frac{k}{8}{e}^{-8x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{8}=1\\,⟹\\,k=8.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d55ee039-24b2-5902-95f1-72edb9d13725"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d55ee039-24b2-5902-95f1-72edb9d13725" :answer-field/id #uuid "d55ee039-24b2-5902-95f1-72edb9d13725" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-54155b31-f5d8-5461-b182-f36b15f21107" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9d4fce9d-6645-5493-9cd5-fa793878e3c5" "answer-febc2157-abdf-56d9-ac62-6e6fbc37e416" "answer-46ff9cf6-5fcf-5d3c-80e8-703c37214290" "answer-4a1b0a3a-bdbd-5374-b104-778a4895bbcf" "answer-54155b31-f5d8-5461-b182-f36b15f21107"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d4fce9d-6645-5493-9cd5-fa793878e3c5" :answer/id #uuid "9d4fce9d-6645-5493-9cd5-fa793878e3c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-febc2157-abdf-56d9-ac62-6e6fbc37e416" :answer/id #uuid "febc2157-abdf-56d9-ac62-6e6fbc37e416" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46ff9cf6-5fcf-5d3c-80e8-703c37214290" :answer/id #uuid "46ff9cf6-5fcf-5d3c-80e8-703c37214290" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a1b0a3a-bdbd-5374-b104-778a4895bbcf" :answer/id #uuid "4a1b0a3a-bdbd-5374-b104-778a4895bbcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54155b31-f5d8-5461-b182-f36b15f21107" :answer/id #uuid "54155b31-f5d8-5461-b182-f36b15f21107" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193868 :tutorial/content "Recall that the **probability density function** (or **pdf**) of a continuous random variable $X$ defined over a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nIt is common for the set $S$ of a continuous random variable $X$ to be an infinite interval. Therefore, we often need to evaluate improper integrals when determining whether a function is a valid pdf and to compute probabilities associated with such a random variable.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following function:\n\n![](images/38/38ea105f5dd0cea7784bf73f814e7161b90cfd224eb099084cc33b6539d95fdd.svg)\n\nIs $f(x)$ a valid probability density function? Let's start by sketching its graph:\n\n![](images/35/3559a998491a004c159683aaf0946bb4fdf87f4afe873fdc93235e840b4b0ac8.png)\n\nClearly, $f(x)\\geq 0$ for all $x,$ so this function satisfies the first condition.\n\nTo check that $f(x)$ satisfies the second condition, we need to compute the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_S f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-x}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nCalculating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-{e}^{-x}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-\\infty }]-[-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =0-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $f(x)$ satisfies both conditions, we conclude that $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nLet's see another example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192931 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
